# Spring Boot / Java 25 — Nexus

Apply these conventions on every change to this Spring Boot service.

## Language and naming

- Write **all** class, interface, method, field, parameter, and local variable names in **English** (camelCase / PascalCase).
- Package folders may keep the existing Spanish package names (`controlador`, `entidad`, `repositorio`, `servicio`).
- Prefer clear verbs for methods: `findById`, `save`, `deleteById`, `listAll`.
- Avoid Spanish identifiers in new or refactored code (`guardar` → `save`, `listarTodos` → `listAll`).

## Java 25 practices

- Target modern Java idioms compatible with Java 25 (records, sealed types, pattern matching, `var` only when the type is obvious).
- Prefer constructor injection; avoid field `@Autowired`.
- Use `Optional` for nullable lookups; do not return `null` from service APIs when an `Optional` or exception is clearer.
- Prefer immutable DTOs and unmodifiable collections at API boundaries.
- Use `java.time` (`LocalDate`, `LocalDateTime`, `Instant`); never `java.util.Date`.
- Prefer `switch` expressions / pattern matching over long if-else chains when appropriate.
- Keep methods focused; extract private helpers for complex logic.
- Mark read-only service methods with `@Transactional(readOnly = true)`.

## Javadoc (required)

- Every **public** method (and public type) must have up-to-date Javadoc: purpose, `@param`, `@return`, `@throws` when relevant.
- Update Javadoc whenever signature or behavior changes.
- Interface methods and their implementations must stay documented (document on the interface; implementations may use `{@inheritDoc}`).

```java
/**
 * Finds a client by document number and type.
 *
 * @param documentNumber the client document number
 * @param documentType the document type
 * @return an Optional containing the client if found
 */
Optional<Client> findByDocumentNumberAndType(String documentNumber, String documentType);
```

## DTOs

- Define DTOs as **Java records** in `com.nexus.nexus.dto`.
- Name them `*Dto` or `*Response` / `*Request` (English), e.g. `ClientReservationDto`.
- Do not expose JPA entities directly in REST JSON responses when a DTO is more appropriate.
- Validate request DTOs with Bean Validation (`@NotNull`, `@NotBlank`, etc.) at controller boundaries when applicable.

```java
public record ClientReservationDto(
    Long clientId,
    String clientFirstName,
    String destination,
    LocalDate tripStartDate
) {}
```

## Layering (follow existing packages)

| Layer | Package | Responsibility |
|-------|---------|----------------|
| Controllers | `com.nexus.nexus.controlador` | HTTP / MVC; no business rules or persistence |
| DTOs | `com.nexus.nexus.dto` | Records for transfer only |
| Entities | `com.nexus.nexus.modelo.entidad` | JPA entities |
| Repositories | `com.nexus.nexus.modelo.repositorio` | Spring Data JPA interfaces |
| Services | `com.nexus.nexus.modelo.servicio` | Business logic; `I*` interfaces + implementations |
| Config | `com.nexus.nexus.config` | WebMvc, interceptors, beans |

- Controllers depend on service interfaces, not repositories.
- Services depend on repositories; keep transactions at the service layer.
- Repositories stay thin (queries only).

## Spring Boot conventions

- Annotate services with `@Service`, repositories with Spring Data, controllers with `@Controller` / `@RestController` as appropriate.
- Use `@Valid` on request bodies/forms that carry constraints.
- Prefer constructor injection:

```java
@Service
public class ClientService implements IClientService {

    private final ClientRepository clientRepository;

    public ClientService(ClientRepository clientRepository) {
        this.clientRepository = clientRepository;
    }
}
```

- Do not log secrets or credentials; keep `application.properties` sensitive values externalized when possible.
- Match existing Thymeleaf template folders when adding MVC views.

## Testing

- Place tests under `src/test/java/com/nexus/nexus/`.
- Prefer meaningful unit tests for services; use `@SpringBootTest` / slice tests when integration is needed.
- Name test methods in English describing behavior.

